{"maxId":288,"entries":{"copy|Other neurodegenerative disorders":["u1"],"neuro-examination|Early symptoms and the eye-movement examination":["u180"],"copy|row:Syndrome|TH|Syndrome":["u181","u214"],"copy|row:Syndrome|TH|Characteristic emphasis":["u182"],"c63-gaze-psp|row:PSP|TH|PSP":["u183"],"c63-gaze-psp|row:PSP|TD|Vertical gaze impairment progressing to palsy with a preserved oculocephalic reflex.":["u184"],"c63-gaze-cbd|row:CBD|TH|CBD":["u185"],"c63-gaze-cbd|row:CBD|TD|Horizontal gaze abnormalities are more typical. The pattern is a diagnostic clue, not a stand-alone diagnosis.":["u186"],"other-dlb|Dementia with Lewy bodies":["u2"],"copy|row:Clinical context|TH|Clinical context":["u3","u18"],"copy|row:Clinical context|TH|Finding":["u4","u19"],"copy|row:DLB|TH|DLB":["u5"],"c63-rbd-dlb|row:DLB|TD|Rapid eye movement sleep behavior disorder occurs in 85% of dementia with Lewy bodies cases.":["u6"],"copy|row:Parkinson disease|TH|Parkinson disease":["u7"],"c63-rbd-pd|row:Parkinson disease|TD|Rapid eye movement sleep behavior disorder occurs in 15% of Parkinson disease patients.":["u8"],"copy|row:Multiple system atrophy|TH|Multiple system atrophy":["u9"],"c63-rbd-msa|row:Multiple system atrophy|TD|Rapid eye movement sleep behavior disorder occurs in 60% of patients with multiple system atrophy.":["u10"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Category|TH|Category":["u87"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Category|TH|Statements supplied from the printed table":["u88"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Probable DLB|TH|Probable DLB":["u89"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Probable DLB|TD|Two core features, or one core feature plus one suggestive feature. Probable DLB cannot be diagnosed from suggestive features alone.":["u90"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Possible DLB|TH|Possible DLB":["u91"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Possible DLB|TD|The supplied page read states: one or more suggestive features.":["u92"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Core features|TH|Core features":["u93"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Core features|TD|Fluctuating cognition with prominent variations in attention and alertness; recurrent visual hallucinations; spontaneous parkinsonism.":["u94"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Suggestive features|TH|Suggestive features":["u95"],"c63-dlb-criteria-table|row:Suggestive features|TD|REM sleep behaviour disorder; neuroleptic sensitivity; low dopamine transporter uptake in the basal ganglia on SPECT or PET.":["u96"],"other-msa|Multiple system atrophy":["u11"],"msa-sleep|Sleep in MSA":["u212"],"other-ftd|Frontotemporal syndromes":["u12"],"ftd-genes|FTD genetics and pathological patterns":["u111"],"c63-grn-c9-contrast|row:Gene|TH|Gene":["u112"],"c63-grn-c9-contrast|row:Gene|TH|Clinical pattern":["u113"],"c63-grn-c9-contrast|row:GRN / progranulin|TH|GRN / progranulin":["u114"],"c63-grn-c9-contrast|row:GRN / progranulin|TD|Often strikingly asymmetric atrophy; later onset than MAPT; varied behavioural, language and motor presentations.":["u115"],"c63-grn-c9-contrast|row:C9orf72|TH|C9orf72":["u116"],"c63-grn-c9-contrast|row:C9orf72|TD|Can begin with a psychiatric presentation; onset commonly in the sixth decade; FTD, ALS or both.":["u117"],"other-language|Progressive language impairment":["u13"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Variant|TH|Variant":["u118"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Variant|TH|Usual pathological association":["u119"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Variant|TH|Single-word meaning and repetition":["u120"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Logopenic (lvPPA)|TH|Logopenic (lvPPA)":["u121"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Logopenic (lvPPA)|TD|Usually Alzheimer disease pathology.":["u122"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Logopenic (lvPPA)|TD|Word retrieval and phonological short-term memory are impaired; repetition of longer phrases is impaired.":["u123"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Nonfluent / agrammatic (nfvPPA)|TH|Nonfluent / agrammatic (nfvPPA)":["u124"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Nonfluent / agrammatic (nfvPPA)|TD|Usually FTLD, most often tau pathology; TDP-43 also occurs.":["u125"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Nonfluent / agrammatic (nfvPPA)|TD|Single-word comprehension and semantic knowledge are relatively preserved; speech is effortful, apraxic or agrammatic.":["u126"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Semantic (svPPA)|TH|Semantic (svPPA)":["u127"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Semantic (svPPA)|TD|Usually FTLD with TDP-43 type C.":["u128"],"c63-ppa-contrast|row:Semantic (svPPA)|TD|Single-word meaning is impaired; repetition, prosody and grammar are relatively preserved.":["u129"],"c63-semantic-ad-memory|row:Syndrome|TH|Syndrome":["u130"],"c63-semantic-ad-memory|row:Syndrome|TH|Typical contrast":["u131"],"c63-semantic-ad-memory|row:Semantic PPA|TH|Semantic PPA":["u132"],"c63-semantic-ad-memory|row:Semantic PPA|TD|Many patients have worse remote than recent memory.":["u133"],"c63-semantic-ad-memory|row:Alzheimer disease|TH|Alzheimer disease":["u134"],"c63-semantic-ad-memory|row:Alzheimer disease|TD|The usual contrast is greater impairment of recent than remote memory.":["u135"],"other-tau|Tau pathology, corticobasal syndromes and PSP":["u14"],"psp-clinical-detail|PSP: gait, speech, eyes and cognition":["u213"],"copy|row:Syndrome|TH|Early course":["u215"],"c63-falls-msa|row:MSA|TH|MSA":["u216"],"c63-falls-msa|row:MSA|TD|Gait instability is common, but falls occur less frequently early than in PSP.":["u217"],"c63-falls-psp|row:PSP|TH|PSP":["u218"],"c63-falls-psp|row:PSP|TD|Nearly all patients in the chapter’s initial descriptions had early gait abnormalities; 60% presented with falls as the first manifestation. Backward falls and pivoting turns are characteristic.":["u219"],"copy|row:Syndrome and mechanism|TH|Syndrome and mechanism":["u220"],"copy|row:Syndrome and mechanism|TH|Speech pattern":["u221"],"c63-speech-msa|row:MSA-C: cerebellar dysarthria|TH|MSA-C: cerebellar dysarthria":["u222"],"c63-speech-msa|row:MSA-C: cerebellar dysarthria|TD|Jerky, intermittently explosive and slurred output, with poor separation of syllables.":["u223"],"c63-speech-psp|row:PSP: spastic dysarthria|TH|PSP: spastic dysarthria":["u224"],"c63-speech-psp|row:PSP: spastic dysarthria|TD|Slurred speech, progressing to mutism in advanced disease.":["u225"],"other-motor-neuron|Motor neuron disease and cognition":["u15"],"ftld-als-direction|FTLD → ALS versus ALS → FTLD symptoms":["u136"],"other-ataxia|Spinocerebellar ataxia":["u16"],"other-seizures-trauma|Seizures and brain injury":["u17"],"copy|row:Pattern|TH|Pattern":["u187"],"copy|row:Pattern|TH|Causal chain and clinical implication":["u188"],"c63-haemorrhage-hypertension|row:Deep haemorrhage / hypertension|TH|Deep haemorrhage / hypertension":["u189"],"c63-haemorrhage-hypertension|row:Deep haemorrhage / hypertension|TD|Hypertension commonly causes haemorrhage in deep structures. Its importance as a cause of haemorrhage does not make it the leading explanation for seizure-associated cortical haemorrhage.":["u190"],"c63-haemorrhage-caa|row:Cortical haemorrhage / cerebral amyloid angiopathy|TH|Cortical haemorrhage / cerebral amyloid angiopathy":["u191"],"c63-haemorrhage-caa|row:Cortical haemorrhage / cerebral amyloid angiopathy|TD|Amyloid in small brain and leptomeningeal vessels → fragile cortical vessels → microbleeds or larger intracerebral/subarachnoid haemorrhage → a cortical substrate for seizures.":["u192"],"copy|row:Mild injury|TH|Mild injury":["u20"],"c63-gcs-mild|row:Mild injury|TD|Severity of head injury is defined by the Glasgow Coma Scale, with 13 to 15 being mild injury.":["u21"],"copy|row:Moderate injury|TH|Moderate injury":["u22"],"c63-gcs-moderate|row:Moderate injury|TD|Severity of head injury is defined by the Glasgow Coma Scale, with 9 to 12 being moderate injury.":["u23"],"copy|row:Severe injury|TH|Severe injury":["u24"],"c63-gcs-severe|row:Severe injury|TD|Severity of head injury is defined by the Glasgow Coma Scale, with less than 9 defined as severe traumatic brain injury.":["u25"],"copy|CHAPTER 63 / HAZZARD":["u26"],"c63-opening|Use the leading syndrome to organize the differential: cognitive fluctuation and hallucinations, behavioural change, progressive language impairment, autonomic failure, gaze palsy, or combined motor and cortical dysfunction.":["u27"],"c63-opening|Diagnostic clues are not interchangeable with complete diagnostic criteria.":["u28"],"c63-ad-context|Alzheimer disease (AD) is the most common neurodegenerative disorder encountered by the practicing geriatrician.":["u29"],"c63-earliest-abnormalities|Examine cognition, behaviour and movement with particular attention to the earliest abnormalities: these often indicate where the disease began and help identify its cause.":["u193"],"c63-history-visuospatial|The cognitive history must assess visuospatial function as well as the other cognitive domains.":["u264"],"c63-history-visuospatial|A memory-only enquiry is incomplete.":["u265"],"c63-saccade-technique|Test saccades in all four directions.":["u194"],"c63-saccade-technique|Ask the patient to fixate a central target, such as a pen tip, then rapidly look at a peripheral target, such as the examiner’s finger.":["u195"],"c63-saccade-technique|Look for delayed initiation, incomplete movement or gaze palsy, and interrupted or jerky movements.":["u196"],"c63-pursuit-technique|Test smooth pursuit separately by asking the patient to track an object horizontally and vertically.":["u197"],"c63-pursuit-technique|Saccadic abnormalities occur across neurodegenerative disorders, from Alzheimer disease to PSP.":["u198"],"c63-dlb-onset|The reported mean onset of dementia with Lewy bodies is 75 years with a range from 50 to 80 years.":["u30"],"c63-dlb-memory|Dementia with Lewy bodies typically involves better performance on episodic memory tasks and recognition memory when compared to Alzheimer disease patients.":["u31"],"c63-dlb-depression|Significant depressive symptoms occur in up to 40% of dementia with Lewy bodies cases.":["u32"],"c63-dlb-motor-frequency|Spontaneous parkinsonism eventually occurs in up to 70% of dementia with Lewy bodies cases.":["u33"],"c63-dlb-motor-pattern|In contrast to Parkinson disease, the parkinsonism of dementia with Lewy bodies is more commonly bilateral and less frequently includes tremor.":["u34"],"c63-dlb-parkinsonian-manifestations|Spontaneous parkinsonism is a DLB core feature.":["u266"],"c63-dlb-parkinsonian-manifestations|Its motor manifestations include rigidity, bradykinesia and hypomimia; hypomimia is a manifestation of the parkinsonism, not an additional independent core criterion.":["u267"],"c63-dlb-common-motor-findings|Common findings include bradykinesia, hypomimia, axial and appendicular rigidity, postural instability and slowed response times.":["u268"],"c63-dlb-mortality-age|Older age is among the factors associated with higher mortality in DLB.":["u269"],"c63-pd-dementia-predictors|Nontremor-prominent PD and a declining response to levodopa are possible predictors of dementia in PD.":["u270"],"c63-pd-dementia-predictors|They are predictive associations, not diagnostic proof.":["u271"],"c63-dlb-fluctuation-history|To elicit cognitive fluctuations, ask specifically about marked alterations in attention, staring spells and episodes of incoherent speech.":["u272"],"c63-dlb-fluctuation-history|These focused questions add detail beyond simply asking whether cognition fluctuates.":["u273"],"c63-cingulate-island|Relative sparing of the posterior cingulate cortical perfusion in dementia with Lewy bodies compared to the occipital cortex is a highly specific finding called the cingulate island sign.":["u35"],"c63-dlb-anticholinergic|Patients with dementia with Lewy bodies should be advised to avoid anticholinergic medications.":["u36"],"c63-dlb-estimates|The following sleep figures are chapter-reported estimates from different disease groups.":["u37"],"c63-dlb-estimates|They are not diagnostic thresholds and should not be applied as universal rates.":["u38"],"c63-autonomic-rbd|When rapid eye movement sleep behavior occurs in association with severe autonomic symptoms, multiple system atrophy rather than dementia with Lewy bodies should be considered.":["u39"],"c63-dlb-timing-chapter61|Chapter 61, TABLE 61-4 (printed p. 941), lists the DLB inclusion criterion as “Dementia within 1 y of the onset of motor features of parkinsonism”.":["u278"],"c63-dlb-timing|Chapter 63 (printed p. 984) states that research criteria for DLB “require cognitive symptoms that predate parkinsonism by 12 months”.":["u279"],"c63-dlb-timing|It describes cognitive deterioration in PDD as occurring in established Parkinson disease, typically years to decades after motor features.":["u280"],"c63-dlb-timing-conflict|The book is not consistent here: it gives the 1-year rule in opposite directions in these two chapters.":["u281"],"c63-dlb-timing-conflict|Chapter 61’s table places dementia within 1 year of motor onset; chapter 63 requires cognitive symptoms to predate parkinsonism by 12 months.":["u282"],"c63-dlb-timing-conflict|Both statements are retained with their own source; they are not reconciled and neither is selected as the operative rule.":["u283"],"c63-dlb-criteria-guide|The table below presents the supplied wording of the printed core/suggestive-feature framework.":["u103"],"c63-dlb-criteria-guide|It differs from the four-core-feature summary in chapter 61; it is not presented as a harmonized diagnostic algorithm.":["u104"],"c63-msa-phenotype-proportions|In the chapter’s account, parkinsonism predominates in approximately 80% of MSA cases (MSA-P), while cerebellar symptoms predominate in about 20% (MSA-C).":["u226"],"c63-msa-phenotype-proportions|These are the chapter’s reported proportions, not a universal distribution for every population.":["u227"],"c63-msa-parkinsonism-pattern|Common presenting features of MSA-P are akinesia, rigidity and postural rather than resting tremor.":["u228"],"c63-msa-parkinsonism-pattern|Autonomic symptoms precede motor symptoms in most cases.":["u229"],"c63-msa-evolution|Early MSA can mimic Parkinson disease, but additional symptoms evolve within 5 years.":["u230"],"c63-msa-evolution|Gait instability is common, while falls are less frequent early than in PSP.":["u231"],"c63-msa-cerebellar-pattern|MSA-C commonly presents with gait ataxia, limb kinetic ataxia and dysarthria.":["u232"],"c63-msa-cerebellar-pattern|Cerebellar speech is jerky, intermittently explosive and slurred, with poor separation of syllables.":["u233"],"c63-msa-autonomic-features|Autonomic manifestations include orthostasis, erectile dysfunction, constipation and urinary incontinence.":["u234"],"c63-msa-autonomic-features|Orthostasis is common, but syncope occurs infrequently.":["u235"],"c63-msa-onset|The mean age of onset for multiple system atrophy is 54.":["u43"],"c63-msa-young-onset|Occurrence of symptoms suggestive of multiple system atrophy in an individual younger than 30 years should raise suspicion for alternative diagnoses.":["u44"],"c63-msa-family-history|A family history of a similar disorder should also prompt consideration of an alternative diagnosis rather than routine attribution to MSA.":["u236"],"c63-msa-levodopa-response|About one-third of multiple system atrophy patients respond to levodopa in the early stages of the disease.":["u45"],"c63-msa-survival|Death in multiple system atrophy typically occurs within 6 to 10 years.":["u46"],"c63-msa-orthostasis|SUPERSEDED CRITERIA FRAMEWORK — the chapter cites a 30 mm Hg systolic fall as its orthostatic diagnostic criterion.":["u237"],"c63-msa-orthostasis|It separately says that a 20 mm Hg systolic or 10 mm Hg diastolic fall is significant in practice when the heart rate does not rise appropriately.":["u238"],"c63-msa-orthostasis|Keep the criterion and practical observation distinct; neither sentence alone is the complete current MSA diagnostic framework.":["u239"],"c63-msa-current-orthostasis|MDS 2022 UPDATE — neurogenic orthostatic hypotension is operationalised as a systolic fall of at least 20 mm Hg, usually accompanied by a diastolic fall of at least 10 mm Hg, with a heart-rate-change/systolic-pressure-change ratio below 0.5 beats/min per mm Hg.":["u240"],"c63-msa-current-orthostasis|Assess within 3 minutes for clinically established MSA or within 10 minutes for clinically probable MSA, on standing or head-up tilt.":["u241"],"c63-msa-orthostasis-exclusions|Exclude alternative causes such as diabetic autonomic neuropathy and medications that blunt the heart-rate response, such as beta blockers.":["u242"],"c63-msa-orthostasis-exclusions|Orthostatic hypotension is one component of the MDS criteria, not a stand-alone diagnosis of MSA.":["u243"],"c63-msa-sleep-rates|Most patients complain of sleep problems.":["u244"],"c63-msa-sleep-rates|The chapter reports sleep fragmentation in 53%, early waking in 33% and insomnia in 20%.":["u245"],"c63-msa-sleep-rates|REM sleep behaviour disorder is common, occurring in up to 60%.":["u246"],"c63-msa-stridor|Nocturnal stridor and obstructive sleep apnoea are frequent.":["u247"],"c63-msa-stridor|Stridor is associated with decreased survival and a risk of sudden death.":["u248"],"c63-bvftd-presentation|The dominance of behavioral and personality changes in the absence of memory and perceptual symptoms is highly suggestive of the behavioral variant of frontotemporal dementia (FTD).":["u48"],"c63-bvftd-frequency|In most centers, behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia accounts for more than 50% of all frontotemporal lobar degeneration cases.":["u49"],"c63-bvftd-age|The mean age of onset for behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia is in the mid-fifties.":["u50"],"c63-bvftd-possible|Recent research criteria for bvFTD require three of six specific symptoms to meet possible criteria.":["u51"],"c63-executive-testing|Executive function tests should not be considered mandatory for a diagnosis of behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia.":["u52"],"c63-bvftd-symptoms-table|The full symptom list for possible behavioural-variant FTD awaits the table contents.":["u53"],"c63-ftd-medications|Do not transfer Alzheimer-specific drug treatment automatically to a frontotemporal syndrome.":["u54"],"c63-ftd-medications|Cholinesterase inhibitors are not routine treatment for FTD and may aggravate behavioural symptoms.":["u55"],"c63-tdp-types|TDP-43 patterns are classified A–D by nuclear and cytoplasmic inclusions.":["u137"],"c63-tdp-types|Type A is associated with progranulin mutations, though sporadic cases occur; type B with motor neuron disease and commonly C9orf72-associated familial FTD–ALS; type C with semantic PPA; and type D with rare valosin-containing protein mutations.":["u138"],"c63-mapt|MAPT was linked in 1998 to familial FTD with parkinsonism (FTDP-17).":["u139"],"c63-mapt|The chapter describes greater frequency in Southern Europe and France than Northern Europe, with a few mutations reported in China and Japan.":["u140"],"c63-grn-frequency|Progranulin (GRN), identified in 2006, lies 1.7 Mb from the tau gene.":["u141"],"c63-grn-frequency|The chapter attributes up to 11% of sporadic and 25% of familial FTD to GRN mutations—different denominators, not a combined prevalence.":["u142"],"c63-grn-onset|GRN-associated disease tends to start later than MAPT-associated disease, with onset described from 35 to 80 years.":["u143"],"c63-grn-onset|Approximately 10% of carriers remain asymptomatic after age 70.":["u144"],"c63-grn-phenotypes|GRN phenotypes are variable: behavioural-variant FTD, nonfluent PPA, corticobasal, Parkinson-like or Alzheimer-like presentations.":["u145"],"c63-grn-phenotypes|Marked hemispheric asymmetry is common.":["u146"],"c63-grn-phenotypes|Autoimmunity is reported in more than 10% of carriers and can precede neurological disease.":["u147"],"c63-c9orf72-mechanism|C9orf72, identified in 2011, is a major genetic cause of familial FTD, ALS and their overlap.":["u148"],"c63-c9orf72-mechanism|An intronic hexanucleotide repeat expansion produces abnormal RNA and dipeptide-repeat proteins through non-ATG translation.":["u149"],"c63-c9orf72-onset|C9orf72 disease commonly begins in the sixth decade, although fourth- and eighth-decade onset occurs.":["u150"],"c63-c9orf72-onset|It may initially resemble psychiatric illness, including bipolar illness, depression, personality or conversion disorder, or addiction; bvFTD, ALS or both may emerge.":["u151"],"c63-ppa-anatomy|In PPA, atrophy is generally greater on the left than the right in the perisylvian region, anterior temporal lobes and basal ganglia.":["u284"],"c63-ppa-anatomy|Inferior parietal lobule atrophy is also described and is associated predominantly with word-finding deficits, termed logopenic aphasia by Gorno-Tempini and colleagues.":["u285"],"c63-ppa-pathology-scope|These are clinicopathological associations, not one-to-one diagnoses: logopenic PPA usually reflects Alzheimer disease, while nonfluent and semantic PPA generally fall within FTLD.":["u154"],"c63-logopenic-phenotype|Logopenic PPA has reduced, slow output with word-finding, naming and sentence-comprehension difficulty.":["u155"],"c63-logopenic-phenotype|Impaired phonological short-term and echoic memory causes parts of longer phrases to be lost during repetition.":["u156"],"c63-nonfluent-speech|Nonfluent PPA produces laboured, apraxic speech, grammatical errors and difficulty with complex syntax.":["u157"],"c63-nonfluent-speech|Apraxia reflects impaired programming of speech-muscle movements: initiation problems, substitutions, omissions, transpositions and distorted sounds; errors are often inconsistent and worsen with multisyllabic words.":["u158"],"c63-nonfluent-language|Anomia varies, phonemic paraphasias are common, articles may be dropped and speech may contain more nouns than verbs.":["u159"],"c63-nonfluent-language|Single-word comprehension and semantic knowledge remain relatively intact.":["u160"],"c63-nonfluent-language|Preserved articulation and grammar with prominent semantic loss argue against this phenotype.":["u161"],"c63-nonfluent-localisation|Speech apraxia points to the left opercular and anterior insular regions.":["u162"],"c63-nonfluent-localisation|MRI often shows left-predominant inferior frontal, insular and caudate atrophy; FDG-PET shows left frontal hypometabolism.":["u163"],"c63-nonfluent-localisation|Insight may remain strikingly good and depression is common; other behavioural problems are less prominent early.":["u164"],"c63-nonfluent-pathological-spectrum|Most nonfluent cases show tau pathology, with corticobasal degeneration more common than PSP and then Pick disease in the chapter’s account.":["u165"],"c63-nonfluent-pathological-spectrum|Approximately 20% show TDP pathology, usually type A.":["u166"],"c63-nonfluent-pathological-spectrum|Emerging PSP, corticobasal or ALS motor features can help predict pathology.":["u167"],"c63-semantic-knowledge|Semantic PPA preserves fluent, grammatically accurate speech while eroding knowledge of words, objects, people and facts.":["u168"],"c63-semantic-knowledge|Semantic knowledge lacks the where-and-when context of episodic memory.":["u169"],"c63-semantic-knowledge|With multimodal agnosia, supplying an object’s name does not restore recognition.":["u170"],"c63-semantic-language|Patients may initially recognise their word-finding and comprehension problems.":["u171"],"c63-semantic-language|Semantic substitutions and vague terms such as “stuff” or “things” become common.":["u172"],"c63-semantic-language|Repetition, prosody, syntax and verb recognition remain relatively preserved despite impaired word meaning.":["u173"],"c63-semantic-testing|Surface dyslexia affects irregularly spelled words such as “gnat”, “heir” and “pint”.":["u174"],"c63-semantic-testing|Category fluency and confrontation naming are impaired, particularly for low-frequency words.":["u175"],"c63-semantic-testing|A right-predominant variant can impair recognition and naming of familiar famous people and resemble behavioural-variant FTD.":["u176"],"c63-semantic-behaviour|Among PPA syndromes, the chapter associates semantic PPA most strongly with behavioural problems: hyperorality, disinhibition, repetitive motor behaviour, reduced sympathy or empathy, narrow interests, parsimony and rigid thinking.":["u177"],"c63-nonfluent-comprehension|In contrast to patients with semantic variant primary progressive aphasia, patients with nonfluent variant primary progressive aphasia maintain single word comprehension and semantic knowledge.":["u56"],"c63-nonfluent-pathology|Approximately 20% of patients with nonfluent variant primary progressive aphasia show TDP pathology.":["u57"],"c63-semantic-memory|In contrast to Alzheimer disease, many semantic variant primary progressive aphasia patients have greater deficits for remote compared to recent memory.":["u58"],"c63-semantic-pathology|Patients with semantic variant primary progressive aphasia often have TDP-43 type C–positive, tau-negative inclusions at autopsy.":["u59"],"c63-ppa-duration|Chapter 63 defines PPA as isolated language symptoms present for at least 2 years in the initial stages of cognitive decline.":["u286"],"c63-pick-frequency|Pick disease occurs in approximately 20% of clinical cases presenting with signs and symptoms of behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia.":["u61"],"c63-pick-tau|Pick disease is usually associated with 3Rtau.":["u62"],"c63-pick-pathology-premise|Pick disease was first described in 1892 by the German neurologist Arnold Pick.":["u274"],"c63-pick-pathology-premise|Its pathological hallmarks are argyrophilic cellular inclusions called Pick bodies and swollen achromatic, tau-positive neurons called Pick cells.":["u275"],"c63-pick-neuron-loss-consequence|Loss of large pyramidal neurons produces a spongiform histological appearance, with selective atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes.":["u276"],"c63-pick-neuron-loss-consequence|Read this tissue-loss finding with the Pick bodies and Pick cells described immediately above.":["u277"],"c63-psp-cbd-tau|Progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration are associated with 4Rtau.":["u63"],"c63-cbd-onset|The mean age of disease onset for corticobasal degeneration is in the mid-sixties.":["u64"],"c63-cbd-pick-bodies|Pick bodies are absent in corticobasal degeneration.":["u65"],"c63-psp-pattern|History of falls and dysphagia with abnormalities in vertical gaze and preserved oculocephalic reflex is suggestive of a diagnosis of progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP).":["u66"],"c63-psp-gaze|Abnormalities in vertical gaze with a preserved oculocephalic reflex are a characteristic finding in progressive supranuclear palsy.":["u67"],"c63-psp-falls|The initial descriptions of PSP emphasized abnormalities in movement with nearly all patients exhibiting early gait abnormalities, and 60% presenting with falls as the first manifestation of disease.":["u287"],"c63-psp-downward-gaze|Downward gaze palsy has greater specificity for progressive supranuclear palsy because mild vertical gaze limitation is normal with aging.":["u69"],"c63-psp-survival|Survival for progressive supranuclear palsy is between 6 and 10 years at the time of diagnosis.":["u70"],"c63-psp-early-series|In the chapter’s initial descriptions of PSP, nearly all patients had early gait abnormalities and 60% presented with falls as their first disease manifestation.":["u249"],"c63-psp-early-series|These figures describe the reported clinical series rather than a requirement for every PSP phenotype.":["u250"],"c63-psp-turns-tone|Patients often pivot when turning and tend to fall backward.":["u251"],"c63-psp-turns-tone|Increased tone is more axial than appendicular.":["u252"],"c63-psp-swallowing-bradykinesia|Dysphagia affects 46% within the first 5 years in the chapter’s account.":["u253"],"c63-psp-swallowing-bradykinesia|By contrast, bradykinesia is described in only about a quarter of autopsy-proven cases; the denominator and case definition matter.":["u254"],"c63-psp-spastic-speech|Spastic dysarthria produces slurred speech; advanced disease can progress to mutism.":["u255"],"c63-psp-vertical-specificity|Vertical supranuclear gaze palsy is the hallmark eye-movement finding and a critical diagnostic feature in the chapter’s classical PSP description.":["u256"],"c63-psp-vertical-specificity|Downward gaze palsy is more specific than upward limitation because mild vertical gaze limitation can occur with normal ageing.":["u257"],"c63-psp-oculocephalic|Early in the disease, the vertical oculocephalic reflex is preserved despite impaired voluntary vertical gaze.":["u258"],"c63-psp-saccade-pursuit|Square-wave jerks, increased latency and hypometria of eye movements may be seen.":["u259"],"c63-psp-saccade-pursuit|Abnormalities are typically greater during commanded saccades than during smooth pursuit.":["u260"],"c63-psp-face|Decreased blink rate and a furrowed brow may be apparent during the interview.":["u261"],"c63-psp-early-cognition|Cognitive or psychiatric features can occur early.":["u262"],"c63-psp-early-cognition|The pattern implicates frontal and temporal lobes and subcortical structures, producing slowed cognitive performance and performance below expectations.":["u263"],"c63-prognosis-context|Survival ranges describe groups in the chapter, not an individual prognosis.":["u71"],"c63-prognosis-context|MSA and PSP share a range here but remain different diseases with different diagnostic findings.":["u72"],"c63-als-incidence|Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis occurs with an incidence of approximately 1 to 2 per 100,000 patients each year.":["u73"],"c63-als-bulbar-onset|Bulbar symptoms are presenting symptoms in about 25% of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis cases.":["u74"],"c63-als-survival|The clinical course of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis has only a 50% survival at 3 years.":["u75"],"c63-ftld-to-als|About 15% of patients with frontotemporal lobar degeneration develop amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.":["u76"],"c63-als-to-ftld|About 50% of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis patients develop cognitive and behavioral symptoms of frontotemporal lobar degeneration.":["u77"],"c63-overlap-context|The direction of the denominator matters: the proportion of FTLD patients developing ALS is not the proportion of ALS patients with cognitive or behavioural symptoms.":["u78"],"c63-als-cognition-scope|Cognitive and behavioural FTLD symptoms in ALS do not all amount to dementia; dementia develops in a smaller subset.":["u178"],"c63-als-cognition-scope|The discovery of C9orf72 changed the earlier emphasis on SOD1 in the genetics of ALS.":["u179"],"c63-ataxia-prevalence|The prevalence of spinocerebellar ataxias is between 1 and 4 per 100,000.":["u79"],"c63-familial-testing|Genetic analyses are estimated to identify only 40% to 60% of familial spinocerebellar ataxia cases.":["u80"],"c63-sporadic-testing|Genetic analyses are estimated to identify less than 25% of sporadic spinocerebellar ataxia cases.":["u81"],"c63-stroke-seizures|Acute and remote strokes are common causes of seizures in older adults.":["u199"],"c63-stroke-seizures|Both haemorrhagic and ischaemic strokes can lead to chronic seizures.":["u200"],"c63-stroke-risk-qualification|The European Stroke Organisation guideline reports higher acute seizure risk after intracranial haemorrhage than after ischaemic stroke, with cortical involvement an important risk factor.":["u201"],"c63-stroke-risk-qualification|That comparison does not establish that most stroke-associated seizures must have a haemorrhagic cause.":["u202"],"c63-caa-protein|CAA involves the amyloid protein associated with Alzheimer disease, with or without parenchymal deposition.":["u203"],"c63-caa-protein|The relevant seizure clue is the cortical haemorrhage pattern, not hypertension alone.":["u204"],"c63-seizure-tumours|Brain tumours are another important cause.":["u205"],"c63-seizure-tumours|The chapter identifies metastases as the most common intracranial tumour type presenting with epilepsy in older people; common primary sites include lung, breast, kidney, colon, rectum and melanoma of the skin.":["u206"],"c63-encephalitis-presentation|Consider autoimmune or paraneoplastic encephalitis when subacute cognitive or behavioural change accompanies seizures.":["u207"],"c63-encephalitis-presentation|Evaluation includes neuronal antibody testing; management may require immunotherapy as well as antiseizure medication, and identification and treatment of any underlying malignancy.":["u208"],"c63-encephalitis-antibody-limit|Antibody testing helps identify the syndrome and associated malignancy, but a negative result does not exclude autoimmune encephalitis.":["u209"],"c63-encephalitis-antibody-limit|Published diagnostic criteria allow antibody-negative cases; a positive result alone is also insufficient without the clinical context.":["u210"],"c63-seizure-infection|Endocarditis can produce septic cerebral emboli or CNS abscesses that present with seizures, a consideration in older adults with valvular disease or prior cardiac surgery.":["u211"],"c63-ad-epilepsy|The rate of epilepsy in Alzheimer disease ranges from 10% to 22% across studies.":["u82"],"c63-penetrating-injury|The risk of development of post-traumatic epilepsy is greater than 50% after a penetrating head injury.":["u83"],"c63-gcs-context|Severity of head injury is defined by the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS), with 13 to 15 being mild injury, 9 to 12 moderate injury, and < 9 defined as severe TBI.":["u288"],"c63-postconcussive-frequency|The prevalence of postconcussive syndrome following brain injury ranges from 30% to 80%.":["u86"]}}
